WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.501 --> 00:00:01.422
- Good morning, Believe Nation.

00:00:01.422 --> 00:00:02.696
My name is Evan Carmichael.

00:00:02.696 --> 00:00:04.189
My one word is believe.

00:00:04.189 --> 00:00:06.780
And I believe that
entrepreneurs will solve all

00:00:06.780 --> 00:00:08.437
of the world's major problems.

00:00:08.437 --> 00:00:11.305
So to help you on your
journey today, our message is

00:00:11.305 --> 00:00:13.705
bet on your strengths.

00:00:13.705 --> 00:00:16.164
Over to you, Gary Vaynerchuk.

00:00:16.164 --> 00:00:18.587
(rooster crowing)

00:00:18.587 --> 00:00:20.686
♫ I wake up every morning

00:00:20.686 --> 00:00:23.137
♫ Espresso keep me going

00:00:23.137 --> 00:00:26.637
♫ I wake up every morning

00:00:27.801 --> 00:00:29.934
- You need to bet on your
strengths, and don't give

00:00:29.934 --> 00:00:31.958
a f-(omitted) about what you suck at.

00:00:31.958 --> 00:00:33.948
Way too many people in this
room are going to spend

00:00:33.948 --> 00:00:36.069
the next 30, 40 years of
their lives trying to check

00:00:36.069 --> 00:00:38.653
the boxes of the things
that they're not as good at.

00:00:38.653 --> 00:00:40.672
And that you're going to
waste a (omitted) load

00:00:40.672 --> 00:00:41.844
of time and lose.

00:00:41.844 --> 00:00:43.800
I highly recommend auditing
yourself, or if you have

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:47.837
no f-(omitted) empathy
or EQ or self-awareness,

00:00:47.837 --> 00:00:49.944
then find somebody in
your family or friendship

00:00:49.944 --> 00:00:52.421
that does, and let them
tell you who you are.

00:00:52.421 --> 00:00:54.450
And once you believe
that, either for yourself,

00:00:54.450 --> 00:00:57.509
or someone else told you,
go directly all chips

00:00:57.509 --> 00:01:00.158
all in to that, because
that is the only possible

00:01:00.158 --> 00:01:03.876
way, in my opinion,
watching from the outside...

00:01:03.876 --> 00:01:06.798
Let me rephrase, that
is a very highly likely

00:01:06.798 --> 00:01:10.242
way of over indexing because the truth is,

00:01:10.242 --> 00:01:14.290
if you want to be an anomaly,
you've got to act like one.

00:01:14.290 --> 00:01:17.435
And so that's it, that's what I got.

00:01:17.435 --> 00:01:18.770
And so thanks for having me.

00:01:18.770 --> 00:01:20.064
(audience laughing)

00:01:20.064 --> 00:01:21.511
- So there's this great
story that I've told before

00:01:21.511 --> 00:01:23.453
but I love it, so I want to tell it again,

00:01:23.453 --> 00:01:25.611
about this rabbit who
goes to animal school.

00:01:25.611 --> 00:01:28.074
And as he's going to animal
school, he learns all

00:01:28.074 --> 00:01:30.109
the skills that he needs
to learn to be a successful

00:01:30.109 --> 00:01:31.530
animal when he grows up.

00:01:31.530 --> 00:01:33.534
And at the end of the
first year, he gets his

00:01:33.534 --> 00:01:34.530
report card.

00:01:34.530 --> 00:01:36.536
And on that report card,
he scores an A plus

00:01:36.536 --> 00:01:40.334
in hopping, and he's
scoring an F in swimming.

00:01:40.334 --> 00:01:42.016
And so his parents come home and they look

00:01:42.016 --> 00:01:43.699
at the report card, and
they start shaming him,

00:01:43.699 --> 00:01:45.807
and they say, how are
you failing in swimming?

00:01:45.807 --> 00:01:47.732
You're a disgrace to the family.

00:01:47.732 --> 00:01:49.281
You got to get trained.

00:01:49.281 --> 00:01:51.555
We're going to hire you
coaches and extra practice

00:01:51.555 --> 00:01:53.957
after school, and we're
going to make sure that you

00:01:53.957 --> 00:01:55.473
do really well on swimming.

00:01:55.473 --> 00:01:57.284
You're really good at
hopping, so don't worry

00:01:57.284 --> 00:01:59.035
about that, just focus on the swimming.

00:01:59.035 --> 00:02:00.856
And so for the next year,
he's focused and focused

00:02:00.856 --> 00:02:02.739
and focused on swimming and
getting better and better

00:02:02.739 --> 00:02:04.491
and better at it.

00:02:04.491 --> 00:02:08.295
And in the process, he forgot how to hop.

00:02:08.295 --> 00:02:09.851
And as for the swimming,
have you ever seen

00:02:09.851 --> 00:02:11.351
a bunny that swam?

00:02:12.765 --> 00:02:14.586
And so this is the
mindset that I think a lot

00:02:14.586 --> 00:02:16.650
of people fall into, where
we get our report cards

00:02:16.650 --> 00:02:19.387
from school or from life,
and we feel that we're

00:02:19.387 --> 00:02:21.774
weak in certain areas,
and our default solution

00:02:21.774 --> 00:02:24.114
for some reason is we
need to work on fixing

00:02:24.114 --> 00:02:25.638
those weaknesses.

00:02:25.638 --> 00:02:28.833
We want to be a well-rounded individual.

00:02:28.833 --> 00:02:30.862
And what ends up happening is, you become

00:02:30.862 --> 00:02:32.963
mediocre at everything,
and those things that

00:02:32.963 --> 00:02:35.429
you potentially can be
world-class genius level

00:02:35.429 --> 00:02:37.429
talent at, you don't do.

00:02:38.728 --> 00:02:41.489
That's not how you have
success as an entrepreneur.

00:02:41.489 --> 00:02:44.179
If you try to do everything
and you only spend

00:02:44.179 --> 00:02:46.333
5% of your time doing the
thing that you could be

00:02:46.333 --> 00:02:49.245
great at, you're always
going to be mediocre.

00:02:49.245 --> 00:02:51.305
You're never going to get
the results that you want.

00:02:51.305 --> 00:02:53.979
Success in business will
come from you focusing

00:02:53.979 --> 00:02:58.358
on what you can be amazing
at, world-class at,

00:02:58.358 --> 00:03:01.878
and spending as much time
doing that as possible.

00:03:01.878 --> 00:03:04.916
And the rest, you eliminate, you automate,

00:03:04.916 --> 00:03:06.587
or you delegate.

00:03:06.587 --> 00:03:08.386
And as quickly as possible,
you want to figure out

00:03:08.386 --> 00:03:10.637
how can I build a team
around me to complement

00:03:10.637 --> 00:03:14.670
my weaknesses so I can go
all in on what I'm great at?

00:03:14.670 --> 00:03:16.258
If I look at my YouTube
channel, at the start,

00:03:16.258 --> 00:03:17.180
I did everything.

00:03:17.180 --> 00:03:18.892
And I'm a big believer in
doing everything to get

00:03:18.892 --> 00:03:19.877
things rolling.

00:03:19.877 --> 00:03:21.398
I hate spending money
'til I'm making money,

00:03:21.398 --> 00:03:24.759
but the first move that I made was to hire

00:03:24.759 --> 00:03:26.215
an editor for my channel, because I wasn't

00:03:26.215 --> 00:03:27.212
great at editing.

00:03:27.212 --> 00:03:29.089
I had no interest in
it, it was not something

00:03:29.089 --> 00:03:31.506
that I ever wanted to
be world-class level at.

00:03:31.506 --> 00:03:33.587
And getting that editor allowed me to go

00:03:33.587 --> 00:03:36.805
from one video a week, to one video a day.

00:03:36.805 --> 00:03:40.001
Seven times my output by
hiring that one person.

00:03:40.001 --> 00:03:42.478
It allowed me to spend more
time at what I was great at

00:03:42.478 --> 00:03:44.847
and wanted to improve at.

00:03:44.847 --> 00:03:46.629
And so when you see
people complaining about

00:03:46.629 --> 00:03:49.456
their weaknesses, I'd
encourage you to think

00:03:49.456 --> 00:03:52.806
and explain to them, don't worry about it.

00:03:52.806 --> 00:03:54.055
What are you good at?

00:03:54.055 --> 00:03:56.284
And spend as much time there as possible.

00:03:56.284 --> 00:03:58.555
When you're doing a
self-analysis, look at what

00:03:58.555 --> 00:04:02.600
do you have the ability to be world-class,

00:04:02.600 --> 00:04:05.283
Michael Jordan-level talent at?

00:04:05.283 --> 00:04:07.844
And then how do you spend 80% of your day

00:04:07.844 --> 00:04:10.517
every day doing that work?

00:04:10.517 --> 00:04:12.496
So the question of the
day today is, I'm curious,

00:04:12.496 --> 00:04:14.797
what is your greatest strength?

00:04:14.797 --> 00:04:16.688
Leave it down in the comments
below, I'm super curious

00:04:16.688 --> 00:04:17.696
to find out.

00:04:17.696 --> 00:04:19.498
I also want to give a quick
shout out to Yael Forman,

00:04:19.498 --> 00:04:21.248
thank you so much for
picking up a copy of my book

00:04:21.248 --> 00:04:23.732
Your One Word, it really,
really, really means

00:04:23.732 --> 00:04:25.797
a lot to me, and I hope
you're enjoying the read.

00:04:25.797 --> 00:04:27.295
Thank you, guys, again, for watching.

00:04:27.295 --> 00:04:29.581
I believe in you, I hope
you continue to believe

00:04:29.581 --> 00:04:32.484
in yourself and whatever your one word is,

00:04:32.484 --> 00:04:34.694
much love and I'll see
you guys again tomorrow

00:04:34.694 --> 00:04:37.853
morning for another shot of Entspresso.

00:04:37.853 --> 00:04:40.085
♫ I wake up every morning

00:04:40.085 --> 00:04:43.585
♫ Espresso keep me going.

00:04:46.588 --> 00:04:48.981
- You need to know what
your unique strengths are

00:04:48.981 --> 00:04:51.149
and be willing to delegate.

00:04:51.149 --> 00:04:53.210
So I would say the question
of whether you should

00:04:53.210 --> 00:04:56.981
take a skill, learn it
yourself, delegate it to

00:04:56.981 --> 00:04:59.865
someone else or hire
a new team member, are

00:04:59.865 --> 00:05:04.849
determined by having a very
accurate self-assessment,

00:05:04.849 --> 00:05:07.395
and assessment of your team's skills.

00:05:07.395 --> 00:05:09.865
Not only what you can do,
what you should be doing.

00:05:09.865 --> 00:05:11.067
That's very important.

00:05:11.067 --> 00:05:13.353
So if you're a startup CEO or founder,

00:05:13.353 --> 00:05:17.103
and you have a team of
say, two to 10 people,

00:05:18.168 --> 00:05:21.691
as soon as you have funds
to hire, you should not

00:05:21.691 --> 00:05:25.126
be focused on the things
that you are bad at.

00:05:25.126 --> 00:05:28.049
Sounds very, very
intuitive, but it is not,

00:05:28.049 --> 00:05:30.077
because you might have
enough time to do those

00:05:30.077 --> 00:05:31.441
things, but the question is,
do you have the attention

00:05:31.441 --> 00:05:35.615
to dedicate to those things so
they're not an interruption?

00:05:35.615 --> 00:05:37.603
Especially if you're a
venture-backed startup,

00:05:37.603 --> 00:05:39.027
and you're competing
against other companies

00:05:39.027 --> 00:05:42.756
that also have a lot of
funding, the game and the race

00:05:42.756 --> 00:05:44.229
goes to the swift, so
you have to have a lot

00:05:44.229 --> 00:05:45.229
of velocity.

00:05:46.664 --> 00:05:48.148
- You talk about managing
your strengths, I know

00:05:48.148 --> 00:05:51.614
one of my weak points
in my personal life is

00:05:51.614 --> 00:05:54.031
I don't like technical stuff.

00:05:55.768 --> 00:05:58.261
So even though I have to
be on Twitter and Instagram

00:05:58.261 --> 00:06:00.877
and Facebook, I don't care for that.

00:06:00.877 --> 00:06:01.781
So guess what?

00:06:01.781 --> 00:06:04.234
I find somebody who loves to do that.

00:06:04.234 --> 00:06:06.215
You know, I find somebody
who has a strength

00:06:06.215 --> 00:06:09.381
where I have a weakness,
and I partner with 'em.

00:06:09.381 --> 00:06:11.928
All you're trying to
do is get to the goal.

00:06:11.928 --> 00:06:13.780
No one gets there by themselves.

00:06:13.780 --> 00:06:15.393
Everybody needs help.

00:06:15.393 --> 00:06:18.075
Now, when you know what
you got goin' for you,

00:06:18.075 --> 00:06:20.075
be confident, not cocky.

00:06:20.996 --> 00:06:23.936
Managing your strengths,
knowing what they are,

00:06:23.936 --> 00:06:26.857
and don't let nobody talk you out of it.

00:06:26.857 --> 00:06:28.787
And that gets you fight ready.

00:06:28.787 --> 00:06:29.718
(audience applauding)

00:06:29.718 --> 00:06:31.342
You get fight ready like that.

00:06:31.342 --> 00:06:32.245
You got to know you.

00:06:32.245 --> 00:06:35.284
Helps you develop your skills.

00:06:35.284 --> 00:06:36.654
- A girl boss.

00:06:36.654 --> 00:06:39.524
A girl boss is someone
who, I guess, takes charge

00:06:39.524 --> 00:06:40.775
of her own life.

00:06:40.775 --> 00:06:42.334
So it's a girl boss or a guy boss.

00:06:42.334 --> 00:06:45.049
It's someone who knows their weaknesses

00:06:45.049 --> 00:06:46.890
and knows what their strengths are

00:06:46.890 --> 00:06:48.602
and knows how to play to their strengths.

00:06:48.602 --> 00:06:50.879
I think there's a lot of people out there,

00:06:50.879 --> 00:06:53.719
and I've interviewed a lot of them who...

00:06:53.719 --> 00:06:54.888
My favorite interview question is

00:06:54.888 --> 00:06:57.544
name your biggest strength
and your biggest weakness,

00:06:57.544 --> 00:06:59.036
or what do you have to work on?

00:06:59.036 --> 00:07:00.740
And the scariest people
are the ones who say

00:07:00.740 --> 00:07:03.327
like, I show up early,
it's my biggest weakness.

00:07:03.327 --> 00:07:06.453
Or like, I work too much,
it's my biggest weakness.

00:07:06.453 --> 00:07:08.800
And, you know, everybody
has things that they can

00:07:08.800 --> 00:07:11.926
improve upon, and I think
a world where everybody's

00:07:11.926 --> 00:07:16.364
more aware of that is
a good one, and a place

00:07:16.364 --> 00:07:19.550
where people can be
empowered to make choices

00:07:19.550 --> 00:07:21.808
in their life rather than throw themself

00:07:21.808 --> 00:07:24.206
into places where they may not inherently

00:07:24.206 --> 00:07:26.421
be successful, because the world has sold

00:07:26.421 --> 00:07:28.454
them some story of what
they should be doing.

00:07:28.454 --> 00:07:32.158
So I guess a girl boss is
someone who's in choice.

